# Leetify Public API key — sent in the `_leetify_key` request header to raise
# rate limits. Get one from https://leetify.com (developer/API settings).
# Copy this file to `.env.local` for local dev. In CI, set the same name as a
# GitHub Actions secret so it gets injected at build time.
VITE_LEETIFY_KEY=

# Optional: URL of the Steam avatar proxy Worker (see worker/README.md). When
# set, the widget shows the player's Steam avatar. This is just a public URL,
# not a secret, so it's safe to inline at build time. For the deployed site set
# a GitHub Actions repository variable named VITE_AVATAR_PROXY_URL. The Steam
# API key itself lives only on the Worker, never here.
VITE_AVATAR_PROXY_URL=

# Optional: PostHog analytics (see src/shared/analytics.ts). Create a free
# project at https://posthog.com, then paste its "Project API Key" (starts with
# `phc_`) here. When set, the customizer loads PostHog (cookie-based, but gated
# behind the consent banner) and the overlay loads it cookieless; leave blank to
# disable analytics entirely (the app works fine without it). The project key is
# a public, write-only identifier, not a secret. For the deployed site set a
# GitHub Actions repository variable named VITE_POSTHOG_KEY.
VITE_POSTHOG_KEY=
# PostHog API host — match your project's region: https://us.i.posthog.com (US,
# the default) or https://eu.i.posthog.com (EU). Set as a repository variable
# named VITE_POSTHOG_HOST for the deployed site.
VITE_POSTHOG_HOST=

# Optional: URL of the Twitch live-status proxy Worker (a separate Worker — see
# worker/README.md). When set, entering a Twitch username makes the win/loss
# session-scoped (resets on go-live, freezes on offline). Also a public URL, not
# a secret. For the deployed site set a GitHub Actions repository variable named
# VITE_TWITCH_PROXY_URL. The Twitch client id/secret live only on that Worker.
VITE_TWITCH_PROXY_URL=
